SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 312 | Monday, March 16, 2026

Good morning.

1) Retail sales up 0.6% in February; inventories flat
The combination points to restocking orders in the second quarter. Furniture and building materials led, which matches the LTL carriers' recent tonnage commentary. Sporting goods and electronics were down; the tariff-driven pull-forward from last spring is still working through those categories.

2) Chicago robotics show: the humanoids are a sideshow, the forklifts are the story
Every booth had a humanoid demo and every operator we spoke with wanted to talk about autonomous forklifts, which are shipping in volume at prices that pencil against a $24-an-hour operator. Three vendors announced pallet-jack robots under $60,000. The piece-picking arms were quieter than at any show since 2019.

3) Commerce publishes the revised chip export rule
The update tightens the license requirement for advanced packaging equipment and adds two dozen entities to the list. Electronics manufacturers with Southeast Asian assembly say the compliance paperwork is now the long pole in their lead times, not the parts.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 18 months. Average age of the planning spreadsheet in use at mid-size manufacturers, per a survey a consultancy released this morning. The oldest respondent reported one from 2009, still in daily use.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

